&ldquo;It&rsquo;s got everything to do with it. Illinois is green, Indiana is
                pink. You show me any pink down here, if you can. No, sir; it&rsquo;s
                green.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, if I was such a numbskull as you, Huck Finn, I would jump over.
                Seen it on the map! Huck Finn, did you reckon the States was the same color
                out-of-doors as they are on the map?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Why, the thing that&rsquo;s happened is that this old bladder has slid
                over Illinois and Indiana and Ohio like nothing, and this is the east end of
                Pennsylvania or New York, or somewheres around there.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, I do, and it&rsquo;s dead sure. We&rsquo;ve covered about fifteen
                degrees of longitude since we left St. Louis yesterday afternoon, and them
                clocks are right. We&rsquo;ve come close on to eight hundred miles.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It grieves me to hear you talk like dat, Mars Tom; I&rsquo;s right down
                ashamed to hear you talk like dat, arter de way you&rsquo;s been raised.
                Yassir, it&rsquo;d break yo&rsquo; Aunt Polly&rsquo;s heart to hear you.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Scriminate! I never heard such ignorance. There ain&rsquo;t no
                discriminating about it. When he makes you and some more of his children black,
                and makes the rest of us white, what do you call that?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Atlantic Ocean&mdash;Atlantic. Land, don&rsquo;t it sound great! And
                that&rsquo;s <i>it</i>&mdash;and <i>we</i> are looking at it&mdash;we! Why,
                it&rsquo;s just too splendid to believe!&rdquo;

&ldquo;You know what that means, easy enough. It means that somebody has got to
                stay on watch and steer this thing the same as he would a ship, or she&rsquo;ll
                wander around and go wherever the wind wants her to.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Wandering,&rdquo; he says, kinder troubled&mdash;&ldquo;wandering,
                without any doubt. She&rsquo;s in a wind now that&rsquo;s blowing her south of
                east. We don&rsquo;t know how long that&rsquo;s been going on, either.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know, and nobody don&rsquo;t know. It&rsquo;s just a word,
                and it&rsquo;s a mighty good word, too. There ain&rsquo;t many that lays over
                it. I don&rsquo;t believe there&rsquo;s <i>any</i> that does.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know what it means, I tell you. It&rsquo;s a word that
                people uses for&mdash;for&mdash;well, it&rsquo;s ornamental. They don&rsquo;t
                put ruffles on a shirt to keep a person warm, do they?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Why, Mars Tom, sholy you can&rsquo;t mean to say I don&rsquo;t know
                about shirts, when, goodness knows, I&rsquo;s toted home de washin&rsquo; ever
                sence&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, it&rsquo;s all right, then; and we&rsquo;ll let bygones be
                bygones. I don&rsquo;t know for certain what a welkin is, but when we land in
                London we&rsquo;ll make it ring, anyway, and don&rsquo;t you forget it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Run! Run fo&rsquo; yo&rsquo; life! Hit&rsquo;s a lion; I kin see him
                thoo de glass! Run, boys; do please heel it de bes&rsquo; you kin. He&rsquo;s
                bu&rsquo;sted outen de menagerie, en dey ain&rsquo;t nobody to stop him!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Now you begin to see, don&rsquo;t you, that <i>distance</i> ain&rsquo;t
                the thing to judge by, at all; it&rsquo;s the time it takes to go the distance
                <i>in</i> that <i>counts</i>, ain&rsquo;t it?&rdquo;
